(Balcony Vegetable Gardening: A Beginner's Guide) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(This short guide was written for the DocuChat sample corpus. It is rendered to a three-page PDF) Tj T*
(so that answers can cite page numbers.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(Choosing a spot) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(Most fruiting vegetables such as tomatoes, chillies and brinjal need at least six hours of) Tj T*
(direct sunlight a day. Leafy greens like spinach, lettuce and fenugreek tolerate partial shade) Tj T*
(and can manage with three to four hours. Before buying any plants, spend one day noting when) Tj T*
(the sun reaches each corner of your balcony.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(Wind is the hidden enemy of high-rise balconies. Strong gusts dry out leaves and snap tall) Tj T*
(stems, so place tall plants against a wall or railing and use a mesh screen on the windward) Tj T*
(side.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(Containers and soil) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(Every container must have drainage holes; waterlogged roots rot within days. A tomato plant) Tj T*
(needs a pot of at least 20 litres, while herbs such as mint and coriander grow well in 3 to 5) Tj T*
(litre pots. Grow bags made of fabric are light, cheap and allow roots to breathe.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(Do not use plain garden soil in pots because it compacts and drains poorly. A good potting mix) Tj T*
(is roughly 40 percent cocopeat, 30 percent compost and 30 percent garden soil or perlite.) Tj T*
ET
endstream
endobj
